<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Hanau-Münzenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1458 CE – 1736 CE · capital Hanau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Main and Wetterau county of the counts of Hanau, whose welcome to Dutch and Walloon refugees in 1597 created the New Town of Hanau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Hanau, raised to counts in 1429, ruled lands around Hanau on the Main and had inherited part of the Münzenberg lordship in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a>. In 1458 the county was divided: the elder line kept Hanau-Münzenberg, while a younger line went on to found the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hanau-lichtenberg">County of Hanau-Lichtenberg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-louis-ii-count-of-hanau-munzenberg">Philip Louis II</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> and in 1597 invited Reformed refugees from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a> to found the New Town of Hanau, which brought gold-working, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other crafts. The county belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-rhenish-circle">Upper Rhenish Circle</a>. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War Hanau withstood a long imperial siege, lifted in 1636.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Münzenberg line died out in 1642 and its lands passed to the Lichtenberg branch. When the last count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-reinhard-iii-of-hanau">Johann Reinhard III</a>, died in 1736, Hanau-Münzenberg went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-kassel">Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel</a>. Hanau later became known as the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1458 CE – 1736 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.72%;width:5.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1458 CE – 1736 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip Louis II, Johann Reinhard III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hanau, Hanau-Münzenberg</dd></dl></aside></div>
